<BaseLayout title="Part I · The Quantum Problem of Matter" description="The coupled electron–nuclear Hamiltonian, its many-particle state, and the Born–Oppenheimer fixed-nuclei problem." current="core">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/core/')}>Core</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Part I</span>
  </nav>

  <p class="secondary">Part I</p>
  <h1>The Quantum Problem of Matter</h1>
  <p class="lede">Before choosing an electronic-structure method, the physical problem is a quantum system of interacting electrons and nuclei. Its compact Hamiltonian hides a state that depends on every particle at once.</p>

  <h2>One Hamiltonian describes all particles</h2>
  <p>Consider a system with nuclei at coordinates <math class="math-inline" aria-label="capital R sub A"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;_A</annotation></semantics></math>, charges <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Z sub A"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_A</annotation></semantics></math>, and masses <math class="math-inline" aria-label="M sub A"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_A</annotation></semantics></math>, together with electrons whose complete variables are <math class="math-inline" aria-label="x sub i equals r sub i comma sigma sub i"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_i=(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;_i,\sigma_i)</annotation></semantics></math>. The many-particle wavefunction is one function of all these coordinates at once:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The many particle wavefunction is a joint function of all particle variables and is not generally a product of separate one electron functions">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="center" rowspacing="0.55em">
          <mtr><mtd><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>≢</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo><mspace width="0.45em"/><mtext>in general.</mtext></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi=\Psi(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;_1,\mathbf&#123;R&#125;_2,\ldots;x_1,x_2,\ldots),\qquad \Psi(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;_1,\mathbf&#123;r&#125;_2)\not\equiv\phi_1(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;_1)\phi_2(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;_2)\quad\text&#123;in general.&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The second line suppresses spin and nuclei only to expose the point. Changing <math class="math-inline" aria-label="r one"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;r&#125;_1</annotation></semantics></math> changes where we evaluate the same joint function of both electrons; it does not merely update an independent trajectory for electron 1. This joint configuration-space object can store interference, entanglement, exchange symmetry, and correlated positions that no list of separate paths contains. A factorized orbital picture can still be a useful approximation, but it must be introduced as a restriction on this more general state.</p>

  <p>What makes this state evolve and assigns energies is the Hamiltonian. Before expanding coordinates, its physical structure is easier to see as five contributions:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Hamiltonian equals nuclear kinetic plus electronic kinetic plus nucleus nucleus, electron electron, and electron nucleus interactions">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mtext>NN</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mtext>ee</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mtext>eN</mtext></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;H&#125;=\hat&#123;T&#125;_N+\hat&#123;T&#125;_e+\hat&#123;V&#125;_&#123;\mathrm&#123;NN&#125;&#125;+\hat&#123;V&#125;_&#123;\mathrm&#123;ee&#125;&#125;+\hat&#123;V&#125;_&#123;\mathrm&#123;eN&#125;&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Two kinetic terms describe the motion of the two particle species. The three Coulomb terms exhaust the pair types available in this model: nucleus–nucleus repulsion, electron–electron repulsion, and electron–nucleus attraction. This decomposition tells us what each later approximation holds fixed or reorganizes.</p>

  <figure class="hamiltonian-map" aria-labelledby="hamiltonian-map-caption">
    <div class="atlas-panel-grid">
      <section>
        <h3>Motion</h3>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Nuclei · <math class="math-inline" aria-label="T N hat"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;T&#125;_N</annotation></semantics></math></dt><dd>Nuclei are quantum dynamical variables in the full problem.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Electrons · <math class="math-inline" aria-label="T e hat"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;T&#125;_e</annotation></semantics></math></dt><dd>A sum of one-electron kinetic operators.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>Coulomb interaction</h3>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Nucleus ↔ nucleus</dt><dd>Repulsion sets a geometry-dependent nuclear contribution.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Electron ↔ nucleus</dt><dd>Attraction ties the electronic environment to nuclear geometry.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Electron ↔ electron</dt><dd>Repulsion couples pairs of electronic coordinates.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </section>
    </div>
    <figcaption id="hamiltonian-map-caption">The five terms classify physical responsibilities before coordinate notation makes the expression look more complicated.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>For the standard nonrelativistic Coulomb model, and using Hartree atomic units, those five objects become</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Full nonrelativistic Coulomb Hamiltonian for electrons and nuclei">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo>
          <mo>−</mo><msub><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mfrac><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac>
          <mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup>
          <mo>+</mo><msub><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac>
          <mo>+</mo><msub><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac>
          <mo>−</mo><msub><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;H&#125;=-\sum_A\frac&#123;\nabla_A^2&#125;&#123;2M_A&#125;-\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;2&#125;\sum_i\nabla_i^2+\sum_&#123;A&lt;B&#125;\frac&#123;Z_AZ_B&#125;&#123;R_&#123;AB&#125;&#125;+\sum_&#123;i&lt;j&#125;\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;r_&#123;ij&#125;&#125;-\sum_&#123;i,A&#125;\frac&#123;Z_A&#125;&#123;|\mathbf&#123;r&#125;_i-\mathbf&#123;R&#125;_A|&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Read the first two terms as motion. The mass <math class="math-inline" aria-label="M A"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_A</annotation></semantics></math> suppresses nuclear kinetic energy relative to electronic kinetic energy, but nuclei are still dynamical quantum degrees of freedom here. The electronic kinetic term is a sum of one-particle operators; by itself it would not couple two electron coordinates.</p>
  <p>The next three terms describe interactions. With fixed nuclear positions, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="V N N"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>NN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_&#123;\mathrm&#123;NN&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> depends on geometry but not on electronic coordinates, so it is a constant during one electronic eigenproblem and must be added back when constructing the total energy surface. The attractive electron–nucleus term depends on both <math class="math-inline" aria-label="r i"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;r&#125;_i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math class="math-inline" aria-label="R A"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;_A</annotation></semantics></math>. Changing nuclear geometry therefore changes the electronic Hamiltonian, the electronic state, and its energy—the basic route from electronic structure to bonding and structure.</p>
  <p>The electron–electron factor <math class="math-inline" aria-label="one over r i j"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/r_&#123;ij&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously depends on two electronic coordinates. That is the genuine many-body coupling: the difficulty is not merely that the sum has many terms, but that moving one coordinate changes the interaction associated with the others. A general eigenstate must therefore store joint information.</p>
  <p>The distances <math class="math-inline" aria-label="R A B"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_&#123;AB&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> and <math class="math-inline" aria-label="r i j"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_&#123;ij&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> are the corresponding pair separations; the pair sums count each like-particle pair once. Hartree atomic units absorb <math class="math-inline" aria-label="h bar e electron mass and four pi epsilon zero"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hbar,e,m_e,\text&#123; and &#125;4\pi\varepsilon_0</annotation></semantics></math> into the units. External fields, relativistic physics, or different electrostatic boundaries extend this standard nonrelativistic Coulomb model rather than changing what its five terms mean.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Martin, <cite>Electronic Structure</cite>, 2nd ed., Sec. 3.1, pp. 60–64.</p>

  <h2>An equation becomes physical through observables</h2>
  <p>A quantum state contains information, but a physical question must still specify what is being asked of it. Energy is represented by the Hamiltonian, a dipole by a dipole operator, and a density at a point by a density operator. For a time-independent Hamiltonian, a stationary state satisfies <math class="math-inline" aria-label="H Psi equals E Psi"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>Ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;H&#125;\Psi=E\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>; that particular operator returns the state's energy eigenvalue. A more general question represented by a self-adjoint operator <math class="math-inline" aria-label="O hat"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;O&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> leads to the expectation value</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Expectation value of O in Psi equals bra Psi O ket Psi divided by bra Psi ket Psi">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi>Ψ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo>
          <mfrac><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>|</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\hat&#123;O&#125;\rangle_\Psi=\frac&#123;\langle\Psi|\hat&#123;O&#125;|\Psi\rangle&#125;&#123;\langle\Psi|\Psi\rangle&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Read the numerator from right to left: the ket names the prepared state, the operator acts on it, and the bra forms the overlap that is integrated over every particle coordinate and summed over spin. The denominator is the state's norm; it becomes one when <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Psi is normalized"><semantics><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\Psi|\Psi\rangle=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The result is the average predicted for repeated preparations of that quantum state.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Martin, <cite>Electronic Structure</cite>, 2nd ed., Chs. 1–3.</p>
  <p>An expectation value need not equal one of the possible outcomes of an individual measurement; it becomes an eigenvalue when the state is an eigenstate of the operator being measured. It is also not a thermodynamic ensemble average, which combines probabilities for multiple states. These distinctions keep later densities, orbital eigenvalues, response functions, and spectra from being treated as interchangeable observations.</p>

  <h2>A restricted trial state changes the reachable minimum</h2>
  <p>The ground state is the admissible state with the lowest energy for the declared Hamiltonian and particle sector. If a normalized trial state is allowed to vary only inside a restricted family, it cannot reach an energy below the true ground-state minimum:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The trial state energy equals its Hamiltonian expectation value and is greater than or equal to the ground state energy">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>Ψ</mi><mtext>trial</mtext></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>Ψ</mi><mtext>trial</mtext></msub><mo>|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>Ψ</mi><mtext>trial</mtext></msub><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>Ψ</mi><mtext>trial</mtext></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>Ψ</mi><mtext>trial</mtext></msub><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[\Psi_\mathrm&#123;trial&#125;]=\frac&#123;\langle\Psi_\mathrm&#123;trial&#125;|\hat&#123;H&#125;|\Psi_\mathrm&#123;trial&#125;\rangle&#125;&#123;\langle\Psi_\mathrm&#123;trial&#125;|\Psi_\mathrm&#123;trial&#125;\rangle&#125;\ge E_0.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The inequality is not a statement that every approximation is accurate. It says that restricting the allowed trial states restricts which minimum can be reached. Enlarging the trial family can lower its best energy, while other observables need not improve monotonically. Part II will make this abstract statement concrete: Hartree–Fock minimizes the same interacting Hamiltonian, but only over single Slater determinants.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Martin, <cite>Electronic Structure</cite>, 2nd ed., Chs. 2–3; the determinant-restricted form is developed in Sec. 3.6.</p>
  <p>The unrestricted state space is difficult precisely because the wavefunction is joint. Even after the nuclei are fixed, an <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N electron"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mtext>-electron</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\text&#123;-electron&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> state depends on <math class="math-inline" aria-label="three N"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3N</annotation></semantics></math> spatial coordinates plus spin, and a modest one-particle representation generates a rapidly growing many-particle space. Practical electronic-structure theories gain tractability by restricting or reorganizing this information—not by declaring that the physical interaction has disappeared.</p>

  <h2>Born–Oppenheimer separation creates an electronic energy surface</h2>
  <p>Nuclear masses are much larger than the electron mass, so nuclear motion is usually slower than electronic adjustment. In an adiabatic regime—where the relevant electronic state remains sufficiently isolated as the nuclei move—we can pause the slower coordinates, treat the complete nuclear geometry <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold R"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> as a parameter, and solve the electronic problem. Repeating that solution for many geometries turns electronic eigenvalues into functions of nuclear coordinates.</p>
  <p>This is a change of problem, not the deletion of nuclear physics. The nuclear kinetic operator <math class="math-inline" aria-label="T N hat"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;T&#125;_N</annotation></semantics></math> is absent from the fixed-geometry electronic eigenproblem because no nuclear coordinate is being differentiated there. It enters the later nuclear-motion problem whose potential is constructed from the electronic solutions.</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Fixed nuclei electronic eigenproblem, adiabatic energy surface, and force">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.62em">
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><msub><mi>Ψ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><msub><mi>Ψ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>NN</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;H&#125;_e(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)\Psi_\alpha(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;;\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)=E_\alpha^e(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)\Psi_\alpha(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;;\mathbf&#123;R&#125;),\quad U_\alpha(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)=E_\alpha^e(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)+V_&#123;\mathrm&#123;NN&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;),\quad \mathbf&#123;F&#125;_A=-\nabla_AU_0(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Read the first line as a family of electronic eigenproblems. For one chosen <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold R"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, the operator contains electronic kinetic energy, electron–electron repulsion, and attraction to nuclei fixed at that geometry. The full electronic coordinate set <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold r"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;r&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> remains dynamical. The label <math class="math-inline" aria-label="alpha"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> selects an electronic state, and solving again at another geometry changes both its wavefunction and eigenvalue.</p>
  <p>The second line restores the nucleus–nucleus repulsion that was constant during each electronic solve. With this convention, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E alpha e"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>e</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_\alpha^e</annotation></semantics></math> alone is not the full adiabatic energy; adding <math class="math-inline" aria-label="V N N"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>NN</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_&#123;\mathrm&#123;NN&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> once produces the potential-energy surface <math class="math-inline" aria-label="U alpha"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  <p>The third line answers an apparent puzzle. “Fixed nuclei” describes how each electronic point on the surface is computed; it does not make the surface flat. Comparing nearby geometries gives the slope of <math class="math-inline" aria-label="U zero"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the negative gradient points in the direction in which the energy decreases. That derivative is the force passed to the later nuclear treatment.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Sources: Martin, Secs. 3.1 and 3.3; Giustino, Ch. 4, especially Eq. 4.9 and the force/equilibrium discussion.</p>

  <figure class="energy-curve" aria-labelledby="h2-curve-title h2-curve-description h2-curve-caption">
    <div class="energy-curve-visual">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="h2-curve-title h2-curve-description">
        <title id="h2-curve-title">Schematic ground-state energy surface of molecular hydrogen</title>
        <desc id="h2-curve-description">From left to right, total energy is high at very short separation, falls to a minimum at the equilibrium separation, and approaches the separated-atom limit. The force is minus the slope of the curve.</desc>
        <line class="axis" x1="62" y1="262" x2="612" y2="262"></line>
        <line class="axis" x1="62" y1="262" x2="62" y2="28"></line>
        <line class="guide" x1="62" y1="103" x2="612" y2="103"></line>
        <path class="curve" d="M78 38 C112 58 139 180 202 220 C244 247 284 208 330 166 C386 116 468 106 600 104"></path>
